Overview Multilingual evaluation pipelines often lose joins between prompts, expected-answer records, and model responses after exports from separate inference and judging systems. A useful audit must restore those joins and preserve the direction of the quality comparison, not merely assign a broad error label to each row. Every case contains four question cards with expected answer points and eight anonymous response cards. Exactly two responses belong to each question. The two responses were produced under different language conditions, but language and model identifiers are removed. Recover the four response pairs and orient each pair from the answer preferred by independent agreement-based evaluation to its weaker counterpart. The result is a directed four-orbit audit graph. Source articles are disjoint across train and test, so memorizing question or answer text from training cannot solve the test boards. Dataset The public split contains 1,200 training boards and 280 test boards. Each board is assembled from real multilingual model responses, gold answer points, and high-confidence evaluator agreements. Original article, item, model, language, response, and judge identifiers are removed. Case-local card identifiers are randomized independently. train.csv Columns | Column | Type | Description | |---|---|---| | case_id | string | Unique anonymized audit-board identifier. | | question_cards | JSON string | Four objects with question_id, question, and an expected_points list. | | response_cards | JSON string | Eight shuffled objects with case-local response_id and answer text. | | orbit_ledger | string | Gold directed pairing-and-preference ledger. | Each expected point has a case-local point_id and its text. Point IDs are informative only inside their own question card. test.csv Columns test.csv contains case_id, question_cards, and response_cards. The target ledger is withheld. Other Files | File | Description | |---|---| | sample_submission.csv | Valid-format placeholder audit ledger for every test board. | Submission Submit columns in this exact order: case_id,orbit_ledger ORB0123456789abcd,Q1>R03>R08;Q2>R01>R06;Q3>R02>R05;Q4>R04>R07 Each atom is question > preferred response > companion response. Atoms must appear in Q1 through Q4 order. Every response ID from R01 through R08 must appear exactly once. Malformed rows receive zero credit without invalidating other rows. Evaluation A valid board defines eight directed edges: four question -> preferred response edges and four preferred response -> companion response edges. DirectedOrbitEdgeF1 is ordinary set F1 over those eight edges, averaged across boards. Directed edges are appropriate because the audit has two operational requirements: restore provenance to the correct question and preserve which answer won the quality comparison. Set-only retrieval would ignore both pairing and preference direction. The expected random-board floor is removed: DirectedOrbitSkill = clip((DirectedOrbitEdgeF1 - 0.13) / 0.87, 0, 1) ExactAuditBoardAccuracy is the fraction of boards whose four directed orbits are entirely correct. Score = 0.60 * DirectedOrbitSkill + 0.40 * ExactAuditBoardAccuracy Directed edge skill rewards recoverable progress in both response grounding and preference direction. Exact board accuracy receives 40% because downstream audit analysis requires all four provenance bindings and all four preference directions to agree. It does not duplicate partial edge credit: it activates only when all eight operational joins and directions are correct. Allowed Methods Train or fine-tune evaluators, cross-encoders, embedding models, assignment models, or constrained decoders using the public training boards. Use general-purpose pretrained language models and public language resources. Apply global matching so each response is used exactly once. Prohibited Methods Do not search for or match response text against the original public evaluation release. Do not use hidden model, language, item, article, response, or judge identifiers. Do not manually annotate test boards or infer labels through coordinated leaderboard probing. &nbsp;
Closes in 3m
